WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.937
Engineering and configuration hunts focus on privileged activity that can alter control-system behavior.

00:00:05.937 --> 00:00:09.978
Relevant evidence may come from engineering workstations, controller logs,

00:00:09.978 --> 00:00:13.570
project repositories, remote sessions, file systems, removable-media records,

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:15.366
and formal change management.

00:00:15.366 --> 00:00:17.162
Start with approved work.

00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:21.652
Identify maintenance windows, assigned engineers, target assets, expected project versions,

00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:23.448
tools, and change tickets.

00:00:23.448 --> 00:00:27.490
Then compare observed connections, files, controller mode changes, downloads,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:30.184
and configuration exports with that authorization.

00:00:30.184 --> 00:00:37.368
Controller logic or parameter changes deserve careful handling because legitimate updates are common and operationally significant.

00:00:37.368 --> 00:00:41.409
Firmware work may also change network behavior or logging.

00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:45.001
Hunters should verify vendor guidance, project records, backups,

00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:49.940
and engineering approval rather than judging a change from one event.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:54.879
Unauthorized project-file movement can include copies to unapproved systems, removable media,

00:00:54.879 --> 00:00:57.124
shared folders, or remote sessions.

00:00:57.124 --> 00:01:01.165
The concern may be integrity, confidentiality, or uncontrolled versioning.

00:01:01.165 --> 00:01:08.349
Preserve file metadata and repository history where available without opening or altering production project files unnecessarily.

00:01:08.349 --> 00:01:11.941
Distinguish suspicious activity from troubleshooting, emergency repair, commissioning,

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:13.288
and routine maintenance.

00:01:13.288 --> 00:01:19.575
Operators and control engineers can confirm whether the process state and observed work align.

00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:24.065
Security teams can correlate identity, endpoint, network, and change evidence.

00:01:24.065 --> 00:01:28.555
Document the exact artifact, source, destination, account, time, approved version,

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:29.902
and process impact.

00:01:29.902 --> 00:01:34.841
If escalation is required, preserve known-good backups and coordinate response authority.

00:01:34.841 --> 00:01:39.780
Unplanned rollback, isolation, or controller reload can create serious operational risk.
